 Digital WAN Support for OpenVMS Clusters

 HIGHLIGHTS

 o  WAN technology using GIGAswitch and FDDI systems provides OpenVMS
    Clusters with DS3 (T3) support; clusters now span 150 miles, six
    times previous FDDI limit

 o  New capability supported by OpenVMS Cluster software for AXP and VAX
    systems, complements new offering of Business Recovery Server

 o  DS3 technology, widely available from public carriers, provides
    eight times bandwidth of Ethernet

 PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

 Many users with operations that span multiple sites have not yet
 optimized key aspects of their business: Expensive resources and
 dedicated staffs may be duplicated at each site. Each regional
 headquarters may have its own set of facilities, or data entry points
 may be at one location and centralized systems at another. Unique or
 expensive resources, such as tape libraries or optical disk archives,
 may be at one site but needed as well at another site. Each site may
 not have access to the latest data.

 Moreover, users often transmit business data between their sites in a
 "batch oriented" style. Orders, inventory, and delivery information are
 often sent in daily transmissions. If a system is down because of a
 system failure or a natural disaster, batched or queued data can be
 lost forever, making it impossible to reconcile business records.

 Business can be more efficient if operated in a "realtime" style. If
 systems in two sites run as a single OpenVMS Cluster configuration,
 critical information can be instantly available between salesroom and
 warehouse, between factory and office, or between data entry point and
 computer center.

 Multisite OpenVMS Cluster configurations, a new feature of both
 VMScluster and VAXcluster software, enable users to operate systems as
 far apart as 150 miles as a single OpenVMS Cluster unit. The support of
 DS3 technology -- commonly called T3 -- makes this possible. Users at
 one site have immediate local access to the latest data, even if it has
 just been updated by users at another site. Multisite technology
 enables users to build an OpenVMS Cluster system spanning greater
 distances than previously possible and thus to optimize their
 operations across regional locations. With DS3, users also have eight
 times the bandwidth of Ethernet, giving them higher performance.
 Additionally, with volume shadowing, storage devices at multiple sites
 can be members of the same shadow set.

 This new cluster enhancement involves the use of a DS3 link, available
 from most telephone service carriers, between the two sites. At each
 end of this link is a DEFGT WAN option card inside a GIGAswitch device.
 This establishes the site-to-site link at distances up to 150 miles,
 delivering a bandwidth of 45 Mbits per second in each direction. At
 each site, the GIGAswitch device is part of the local FDDI link or dual
 FDDI ring.

 This new feature is an enhancement of the VMScluster Software for
 OpenVMS AXP and VAXcluster Software for OpenVMS VAX products. It is not
 a new product requiring a separate license. Each installed OpenVMS
 Cluster system can take advantage of this capability. Users with
 installed OpenVMS Cluster systems can determine whether clustering with
 another part of their organization within a 150 mile radius would
 increase their efficiency.

 BENEFITS

 o  Multisite OpenVMS Cluster systems bring the well-established
    benefits of clustering -- such as high availability, consistent
    system management, and transparent access to data -- into a new
    business environment. The combination of cluster functionality and
    WAN distances gives users a more cost effective way to run their
    business. This is the most tightly coupled wide area system
    architecture in the industry, with 100 percent data integrity at
    each site at the completion of each individual transaction.

 o  Instead of requiring support staff at multiple locations, users will
    need fewer staff to manage their multisite OpenVMS Cluster system.
    Their personnel can be deployed on other MIS activities and deliver
    other end-user services.

 o  Users wishing to have data replicated at remote sites with 100
    percent accuracy and realtime availability can make these sites part
    of their OpenVMS Cluster system without requiring new network
    operational procedures.

 o  Previously, users could connect sites up to 25 miles apart with FDDI
    technology. Sites that exceeded the 25 mile limitation typically
    used Ethernet technology with its 10 Mbit per second bandwidth. DS3
    technology delivers the best of both technologies: eight times the
    bandwidth of Ethernet at distances up to six times the FDDI limit.

 o  DS3 technology may have the added benefits of using existing wiring
    and being available in more areas than FDDI.

 o  Examples of installations that benefit from multisite OpenVMS
    Cluster systems using DS3 technology include:

    -  Engineering workstations in San Francisco using realtime data
       from a corporate datacenter in Sacramento.
    -  Users in Cleveland requiring access to centralized resources,
       such as archives, optical libraries, or supercomputers in
       Pittsburgh.
    -  Order processing stations in Boston accessing inventory data in a
       warehouse in Portland, Maine.
    -  Workers in New York City requiring current data, up to and
       including the very last transaction, that was generated and
       stored in Albany.
    -  Users in cities where a specific activity is concentrated -- such
       as financial trading in New York...insurance in Hartford...
       hospitals in Houston... -- can access data in an absolute
       realtime and 100 percent available manner.

 o  Users in all these examples not only benefit from access to the
    latest data and sharing of unique resources, but also have their
    systems managed and operated as a common system. For example, the
    following pairs of cities are located within 150 miles of each other
    and could establish multisite OpenVMS Cluster systems:

    Washington-Baltimore             Boston-Providence
    Philadelphia-Atlantic City       Boston-Concord NH
    Philadelphia-Dover               Tampa-Orlando
    Providence-Hartford              San Francisco-Sacramento
    Baltimore-Dover                  Birmingham-Montgomery
    Washington-Dover                 Portland ME-Concord NH
    Louisville-Cincinnati            Chicago-Milwaukee
    Boston-Hartford                  Philadelphia-Baltimore
    Buffalo-Toronto                  New York-Philadelphia
    Denver-Cheyenne                  Washington-Richmond
    Norfolk-Richmond                 Indianapolis-Cincinnati
    Cincinnati-Columbus              Indianapolis-Louisville
    Hartford-Albany                  Boston-Portland ME
    New York-Hartford                Los Angeles-San Diego
    Washington-Philadelphia          Omaha-Des Moines
    Little Rock-Memphis              New York-Albany
    Pittsburgh-Cleveland             Seattle-Vancouver
    New Orleans-Mobile               Cleveland-Columbus
    New York-Atlantic City           Boston-Worcester

 AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

 The WAN support for OpenVMS Clusters described in this article is
 available now.

 Digital recommends that if users are combining datacenters for disaster
 tolerance, they should use the Business Recovery Server to ensure
 successful implementation.

 Users making use of GIGAswitch and FDDI technology need the following
 components:

 DEFGT-AA     GIGAswitch 2 port linecard (motherboard) for DS3/SONET/ATM
 DEFGE-AA     DS3 45 Mbit per second card (daughterboard) for DEFGT-AA

 TECHNICAL DATA

 Software Prerequisites

 o  For Alpha AXP systems: OpenVMS AXP operating system and the
    VMScluster Software for OpenVMS AXP product.
 o  For VAX systems: OpenVMS VAX operating system and the VAXcluster
    Software for OpenVMS VAX product.

 Optional Software

 o  Volume Shadowing for OpenVMS VAX systems.
 o  Volume Shadowing for OpenVMS AXP systems.

 Restrictions

 o  Support for DS3 described in this article is limited to areas in
    which DS3 is available. The E3 service available in Europe is not
    supported by the hardware described here. Later this year, OpenVMS
    Cluster systems spanning increased distances with SONET/ATM
    technology will be supported in all areas.



