PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

The MicroVAX 3100 Model 85 meets user requirements for client/server desktop
computing solutions that are quality driven, performance rich, and priced
right. Engineered to handle demanding computing tasks in open office
environments, this newest member of the MicroVAX family is an ideal choice
for most businesses that require graphics oriented, high volume, low cost,
distributed computing. As an ADVANTAGE-SERVER with its newly packaged,
built-in software components, it combines the power of the MicroVAX family,
the robustness of the OpenVMS operating system, and the dependability of the
mature software to provide a powerful system to users of desktop and
rackmountable servers.

Based on a 16 nanosecond NVAX CMOS4 chip design, the Model 85 can achieve up
to 110 TPS (transactions per second), compared with 60 TPS for the Model 80
it is replacing. Both commercial and technical applications can benefit from
the increase in processing power from 10 to 16 VUPS. The applications can be
developed and deployed on the same affordable system with better-than-ever
performance.

Other key features:

o  Improved Ethernet speed based on a faster SGEC chip.
o  Increased disk capacity, to 29.4 Gbytes.
o  Increased error correction code (ECC) memory, to 128 Mbytes.
o  Support for second optional SCSI adapter (KZDDA).
o  Support for OpenVMS V6.1 and V5.5-2H4.
o  Enhanced support for base systems, packaged systems, and upgrades.
o  Continued support for BA42B-AA tabletop enclosure.
o  Rackmountable support for specialized configurations.

When used as a desktop ADVANTAGE-SERVER, the Model 85 delivers a range of
desktop integration solutions. Complete with NAS 200 Services, the server is
both network ready (for DECnet, TCP/IP, and PATHWORKS based networks) and
client/server rich (with DECwindows Motif, ObjectBroker, DECmessageQ, and
POLYCENTER Distribution client software). Users can thus connect MS-DOS,
OS/2, UNIX, and Macintosh clients to share information throughout their
organization.

For further product information, consult the August supplement of the
Systems and Options Catalog.

